\[
\text{Speed on beach} = \frac{2 \text{ miles}}{40 \text{ minutes}} = \frac{1}{20} \text{ miles per minute}
\]

Next, her speed on the sidewalk is twice that:

\[
\text{Speed on sidewalk} = 2 \times \frac{1}{20} = \frac{1}{10} \text{ miles per minute}
\]

Now, calculate the time taken for each part of the walk:

- Time on beach: Already given as 40 minutes.
- Time on sidewalk: 
\[
\text{Time} = \frac{\text{Distance}}{\text{Speed}} = \frac{1 \text{ mile}}{\frac{1}{10} \text{ miles per minute}} = 10 \text{ minutes}
\]

Finally, add the times for all parts:

\[
40 \text{ minutes (beach)} + 10 \text{ minutes (sidewalk)} = 50 \text{ minutes}
\]

